in hong kong's shrinking media space  another loss in a statement on social  media independent online news site  citizen news said it would stop  operating from tuesday saying it needed  to ensure the safety of its staff it  described itself as a little boat in the  middle of tremendously rough seas  that announcement came less than a week  after stan news announced it would close  down hours after the independent  publication had been raided by police  and seven people were arrested in  october hong kong's security bureau  accused citizen news of misleading its  readers when reporting that free speech  was no longer guaranteed under the  national security law  founder chris young said although  citizen news had not been contacted by  national security police it was  impossible for the small outlet to  continue  overall media is facing a  an increasingly tough  environment  for those who are being seen as  critical or troublemakers they are more  vulnerable young said he hoped young  hong kong journalists wouldn't be put  off saying there's still a role for  media to play in the territory a  sentiment echoed by other journalists  there have been a lot of media workers  who expressed their sadness on social  media and i don't think we are the only  ones who suffered in this industry  there are still a lot of journalists who  are willing to try hard so i still have  hope citizen news is the third outlet to  shut down after stand news and hong  kong's last pro-democracy paper apple  daily that was forced to close in june  last year after its assets were seized  and founder jimmy lai arrested  authorities have also blocked programs  on public broadcaster radio television  hong kong and issued directives to staff  it's now uncertain how remaining outlets  including hong kong free press can  survive the decision by citizen news to  close is another sign that the media  environment here is becoming  increasingly difficult yet hong kong  authorities have repeatedly rejected  claims of a crackdown saying they are  not targeting the media journalism is  not sufficient but seditious acts and  activities  and inciting  other people through public access  activities could not be condoned  under the guise of news reporting hong  kong's chief secretary john lee  criticized a recent editorial published  in the wall street journal titled no one  is safe in hong kong saying that recent  arrests had nothing to do with media  freedom  hong kong was once known as a vibrant  hub for media outlets but a national  security law imposed on the region in  2020 has changed that and left many  fearing independent journalism is being  crushed britt clennan al-jazeera hong  kong
 

